.TH DECK 1
.SH NAME
deck, Deck \- play sound, with a scrub bar
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B deck
[
.B -p
] [
.I file ...
]
.PP
.B Deck
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Deck
plays a list of sound files with a scrub bar.
.I deck
plays them in the window it is run in;
.IR Deck ,
in the tag of an
.IR acme (1)
window, plays the files the window's lines name, the window's text being the playlist.
.PP
.I Deck's
lines name files by their paths, or by paths from the window's directory,
so
.I Deck
in the tag of a directory's window plays the directory.
Given
.IR file ,
it opens a window on it instead.
Empty lines, lines that begin with
.BR # ,
as an M3U playlist's comments do,
and names of directories are passed by, and a line of names separated by tabs,
as acme shows a directory, is a name each.
It opens on the line at the window's selection, decoded and paused,
and sets the selection to each track it plays.
.PP
.I deck
plays its
.I files
in turn, a directory's files in order of their names.
With
.BR -p ,
as the plumber starts it,
it plays the files plumbed to the
.B audio
port, each from its place in the list, added at the end if it is not there.
Only one
.B -p
.I deck
runs at a time.
.PP
Both draw the same picture.
At the top are the track's name and, at the right,
where it is and how long it is, with
.B paused
or
.B wait
before them when it is paused or waiting for the decoder.
A length decoded only in part is an estimate, marked
.BR ~ .
Below them is the scrub bar, the loudness of the track drawn as it is decoded,
the part played in the
.B hold
color.
Below the bar is the playlist, with the line being played highlighted.
.PP
A track is decoded into memory by the decoder of
.IR audio (1)
that its name's extension calls for, or by
.IR play (1),
faster than it plays, so a jump anywhere already decoded is at once.
A track that fails to decode is passed over.
.I Deck
opens
.B /dev/audio
while it plays and closes it when it pauses, so another program can play in the meantime.
.SS Mouse
.TP
Button 1 on the bar
Jumps there.
Dragging plays from under the pointer as it goes, so you hear where you are,
and releasing plays on from there, or stays paused if it was paused.
With the pointer more than a line above or below the bar, a drag moves at half the pointer's speed,
a quarter two lines away and an eighth three or more,
for fine work in a long track; the readout shows the speed.
.TP
Button 1 on a line
Plays that track.
.TP
Button 2
Plays or pauses.
.TP
Button 3
A menu:
.B Play
or
.BR Pause ,
.BR Next ,
.B Prev
and
.BR Exit .
.TP
The wheel
Over the bar or above it, jumps 5 seconds back for up and on for down, as text scrolls;
over the playlist, scrolls it.
.PP
With the pointer over the bar, the readout shows the time under it.
.SS Keys
.TP
.B space
Plays or pauses.
.TP
.B ← →
Jump 5 seconds back or on.
.TP
.B ↑ ↓
Play the track before or after.
.TP
.B 0 \fRto\fP 9
Jump to that tenth of the track.
.SS "Deck's commands"
.TP
.B Play Pause Next Prev
in the tag do as their names say.
.TP
.B Deck
in the tag ends
.I Deck
and shows the body again.
.TP
.B Del
ends
.I Deck
with its window.
.SH EXAMPLES
Play a directory:
.IP
.EX
deck /usr/glenda/music
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /tmp/deck.plumb.\fIuser\fP
.TP
.BI /tmp/deck.plumb. user
held by the
.B -p
.I deck
.TP
.B /sys/lib/plumb/basic
the rule that plumbs sound to
.I deck
.SH SOURCE
.B src/cmd/deck
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR play (1),
.IR audio (1),
.IR plumber (4),
.IR acme (1),
.IR audio (3)
.SH BUGS
A key's or the wheel's jump past what is decoded waits there for the decoder, shown as
.BR wait ;
a click or a drag on the bar goes no further than what is decoded.
A pause lets the fifth of a second the device holds play out.
